---
import seal from '../../design/reference/widgets/wax_letters.png';
import plate from '../../design/reference/heros/cartographers_desk_vign.png';
import Landing from '../components/Landing.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="About"
  description="Miraforge is an independent software company building tools for writers and other creative professionals."
>
  <Landing
    title="Built for the work of creating"
    lead="Miraforge is an independent software company building tools for writers and other creative professionals."
    hero
    seal="/assets/optimized/wax_letters__200w.webp"
    plate="/assets/optimized/cartographers_desk_vign__1920w.webp"
    plateAlt="A journal, pen, and brass instruments on a writer's desk."
  >
    <Fragment slot="essay">
      <p>
        We started Miraforge because we are writers ourselves, and we grew tired of assembling a
        creative workspace from software made by different companies for different purposes. Each
        tool might do its job well, but moving constantly between them breaks the creative rhythm.
        Manuscripts become separated from notes. Characters and places live somewhere else.
        Timelines, research, and ideas scatter across applications, files, and services.
      </p>
      <p>Over time, the work itself becomes fragmented.</p>
      <p>
        We wanted something different: a place where the manuscript and everything that gives it
        context could live together, without the software getting in the way of the writing.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also wanted confidence that our work remained ours. Creative projects can represent
        years of thought and effort. They shouldn't depend on the continued existence of a
        particular cloud service or company. Writers should be able to understand where their work
        lives, keep control of it, and trust that the tools they use today won't become a barrier
        to their work tomorrow.
      </p>
      <p>That thinking became Miraforge Studio.</p>
      <h2>Technology should serve the writer</h2>
      <p>
        The arrival of generative AI added another question: what role, if any, should AI have in a
        creative workspace?
      </p>
      <p>
        We understand why that question is divisive. Miraforge isn't built around AI, and AI isn't
        required to use Studio. We began with the traditional tools writers actually need: writing,
        organization, lore, timelines, structure, relationships, and the ability to understand a
        growing body of creative work.
      </p>
      <p>But we also believe AI can be useful when it is applied with restraint and purpose.</p>
      <p>
        We aren't interested in bolting a general-purpose chatbot onto a manuscript or asking a
        machine to become the author. We are interested in the less glamorous problems: finding
        information buried in years of notes, checking continuity, organizing material, handling
        repetitive work, and helping a writer navigate the growing complexity of a project without
        losing focus.
      </p>
      <p>Our goal is not AI that demands your attention.</p>
      <p>It is technology that quietly removes obstacles from your path.</p>
      <h2>Keep creating</h2>
      <p>Miraforge exists to help creative people spend more of their time creating.</p>
      <p>
        That means building tools that work together instead of pulling you in different
        directions. It means respecting ownership of the work and the choices of the person
        creating it. And it means adopting new technology when it genuinely makes the creative
        process better, not simply because it is new.
      </p>
      <p>We aren't pro-AI or anti-AI.</p>
      <p class="credo">We're pro-creator.</p>
      <p>That's the standard we're building Miraforge around.</p>
      <div class="landing-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary btn-brass" href="/">Return home</a>
        <a class="btn btn-brass-inverse" href="/studio/">Explore Studio</a>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Landing>
</Base>
